  "post": {
    "caption": "Diamonds and pencil lead share one element. Both are pure carbon, and only the arrangement of the atoms differs. In diamond, each atom bonds to four others in a rigid lattice. In graphite, each atom bonds to three others in flat sheets, and those sheets slip past each other, so graphite is soft. Pencil lead has no lead in it because graphite was once mistaken for lead. Diamond is 10 on the Mohs scale and graphite is about 1 to 2. Natural diamonds form deep underground, more than 100 kilometers down, and some labs grow diamonds from graphite using high pressure and heat. A diamond conducts heat very well, and heated enough in oxygen it burns and becomes carbon dioxide.",
